# Per-account topic access, checked against the accounts in passwd (built by
# init-passwd.sh from the *_MQTT_PASS variables in .env). Nothing in this
# file is a secret -- usernames and topic shapes only.

# Applies to every ANONYMOUS client (mosquitto.conf sets allow_anonymous
# true) -- rules with no preceding `user` line are global, but only for
# clients that never authenticate. Verified live (mosquitto 2.1.2):
# authenticating with a username drops a client out of this rule entirely,
# whether or not that username has its own `user` block below -- mosquitto
# does not union global rules into an authenticated client's grants, it
# replaces them. Every account below must therefore grant itself every
# topic it needs to read explicitly; nothing here is inherited.
# Read-only, and deliberately covers everything under energy/# with no
# exceptions -- including energy/+/set, so anyone on the LAN can watch
# inverter commands go by with no account at all. That's intentional, not
# an oversight: reads never need a password here, only writes do (below).
topic read energy/#

# poller (app/poller.py): publishes its own raw state, command results, and
# derived state (PV correction/energy integration/bank aggregation --
# app/derive.py -- and the solar forecast, app/forecast.py, all computed
# in-process now rather than by a separate Node-RED flow). Listens for
# commands -- needs its own read grant on energy/+/set (see the note
# above: authenticating opts this account OUT of the global rule, it does
# not inherit it).
user poller
topic read energy/+/set
topic write energy/+/state
topic write energy/+/set/result
topic write energy/derived/#

# settings: solar_settings.html's account. Publishes inverter commands, and
# also has to read back the derived state it displays and the write
# results it confirms -- same reasoning as poller above, this account gets
# nothing from the global rule just by existing. solar_dash.html and
# config.html don't need any of this: they connect with no MQTT account at
# all, so they stay genuinely anonymous and the global rule above covers
# them fully.
user settings
topic read energy/derived/+
topic read energy/+/set/result
topic write energy/+/set
